  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": " A shoot 屮 growing in the soil 土"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a sprout emerging from the ground."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "進也。象艸木生出土上。凡生之屬皆从生。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Ideogrammic compound (會意 /会意): 屮 (“bud”) + 一 (“ground”) – sprouting from the ground. A conservative version is 𤯓, which is unrelated to 㞷 (匩 > 匡) and 𡴀 > 丰.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *sreŋ (“to live; to be alive; to give birth; raw; green”). Cognate with Burmese ရှင် (hrang, “to live; alive”), Mizo hring (“to bear; to bring forth; to give birth to; green”).\nSchuessler (2007) proposes that Proto-Sino-Tibetan *sriŋ is derived from the root *sri (“to exist”) (whence possibly Chinese 體 (OC *r̥ʰiːʔ, “body; shape; form”)) + *-ŋ (terminative suffix).\nBoth level tone and falling tone readings are found in Middle Chinese, but the latter has since been lost and is merged into the level-tone reading in modern dialects.\nRelated to 青 (OC *sʰleːŋ, “blue-green”), 蒼 (OC *sʰaːŋ, *sʰaːŋʔ, “dark blue; deep green”).\nDerivatives: 性 (OC *sleŋs, “nature; character; personality; quality”), 姓 (OC *sleŋs, “family name”).\nPronunciation in Early Middle Chinese is *sraeng, but it comes from an earlier *srjaeng, as seen in Wang Renxu's edition of the Qieyun."
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "From Mandarin 上."
    }
  ],
